          "answer": "**Seek emergency care now.** What you have described can be a medical emergency. Call emergency services or go to an emergency department immediately. Do not wait to see whether it improves, and do not drive yourself.\n\nA father having a heart attack at 55 means you have a family history of early-onset heart attack, which increases your personal risk for cardiovascular disease (consultation). This is considered a \"risk-enhancing feature\" (consultation, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation).\n\nWhen deciding whether to prescribe a statin, clinicians first estimate an individual's 10-year risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (consultation). This estimate uses factors like age, sex, cholesterol levels, blood pressure, and smoking status (consultation). Your family history of premature cardiovascular disease then acts as a risk-enhancing feature (consultation, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation). This can help guide the decision to start statin therapy, particularly if your estimated 10-year risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease is in the borderline (5% to less than 7.5%) or intermediate (7.5% to less than 20%) categories (consultation, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation). In these cases, the presence of a risk-enhancing feature like a family history of premature heart attack could favor initiating statin therapy (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation\").\n\n[removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.]\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
